1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,404
<font color="#000000">Graph neural networks are limited in what they can
compute, and the yardstick,</font>

2
00:00:05,484 --> 00:00:07,449
<font color="#000000">the Weisfeiler-Lehman hierarchy,</font>

3
00:00:07,529 --> 00:00:11,410
<font color="#000000">is hard to interpret and too coarse to separate
architectures.</font>

4
00:00:11,490 --> 00:00:17,928
<font color="#000000">This ICML 2023 paper measures a graph network
instead by which equivariant polynomials it can compute.</font>

5
00:00:18,008 --> 00:00:21,506
<font color="#000000">The authors give a complete basis for these polynomials,</font>

6
00:00:21,586 --> 00:00:24,573
<font color="#000000">build tools to read off any model's exact power,</font>

7
00:00:24,653 --> 00:00:29,940
<font color="#000000">and design a stronger network that reaches state of
the art on molecular benchmarks.</font>

8
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:36,596
<font color="#000000">Graph networks have bounded expressive power: only
some functions of a graph can be represented.</font>

9
00:00:36,676 --> 00:00:42,025
<font color="#000000">The field measures this with the Weisfeiler-Lehman
hierarchy of isomorphism tests.</font>

10
00:00:42,105 --> 00:00:46,593
<font color="#000000">But WL is combinatorial, giving no recipe to make a
network stronger,</font>

11
00:00:46,673 --> 00:00:52,088
<font color="#000000">and its rungs are so far apart that very different
GNNs collapse to the same level.</font>

12
00:00:52,168 --> 00:00:54,670
<font color="#000000">We need a finer, more actionable ruler.</font>

13
00:00:55,050 --> 00:01:00,654
<font color="#000000">The authors argue the right way to grade a graph
network is by the quantities it can compute.</font>

14
00:01:00,734 --> 00:01:02,121
<font color="#000000">Equivariant polynomials,</font>

15
00:01:02,201 --> 00:01:06,094
<font color="#000000">polynomials of the adjacency matrix that respect
node relabeling,</font>

16
00:01:06,174 --> 00:01:12,084
<font color="#000000">are natural candidates: subgraph counts and many
structural features are exactly such polynomials.</font>

17
00:01:12,164 --> 00:01:15,690
<font color="#000000">Knowing their full space, and which a network can
evaluate,</font>

18
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:20,030
<font color="#000000">gives both a fine-grained ruler and a to-do list of
missing operations.</font>

19
00:01:20,410 --> 00:01:24,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core object is a basis for equivariant graph
polynomials.</font>

20
00:01:24,577 --> 00:01:29,143
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each basis element is a directed multi-graph with a
marked red edge,</font>

21
00:01:29,223 --> 00:01:31,739
<font color="#FFFFFF">and its value is a tensor contraction,</font>

22
00:01:31,819 --> 00:01:36,043
<font color="#FFFFFF">an einsum over adjacency entries that sums over
internal nodes.</font>

23
00:01:36,123 --> 00:01:40,825
<font color="#FFFFFF">Averaging monomials over all node permutations, the
Reynolds operator,</font>

24
00:01:40,905 --> 00:01:42,396
<font color="#FFFFFF">makes them equivariant.</font>

25
00:01:42,476 --> 00:01:43,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">To analyze a network,</font>

26
00:01:43,911 --> 00:01:49,911
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors treat it as a bank of primitive
contractions and give a linear-time algorithm</font>

27
00:01:49,991 --> 00:01:52,848
<font color="#FFFFFF">deciding which polynomials it can assemble.</font>

28
00:01:52,928 --> 00:01:58,314
<font color="#FFFFFF">Node-based banks equal one-WL, edge-based banks
equal two-FWL, that is three-WL.</font>

29
00:01:58,394 --> 00:02:03,369
<font color="#FFFFFF">Building on this, PPGN plus plus feeds in
precomputed polynomial features,</font>

30
00:02:03,449 --> 00:02:05,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">lifting it strictly above three-WL.</font>

31
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:11,303
<font color="#000000">The result is state of the art across the board.</font>

32
00:02:11,383 --> 00:02:16,546
<font color="#000000">On ZINC-12K,</font>

33
00:02:16,626 --> 00:02:21,789
<font color="#000000">PPGN plus plus with degree-six polynomial features
reaches 0.071 test error,</font>

34
00:02:21,869 --> 00:02:27,031
<font color="#000000">beating CIN at 0.079 and GIN at 0.163.</font>

35
00:02:27,111 --> 00:02:32,274
<font color="#000000">On ZINC-full it hits 0.020 and on Alchemy 0.109,
both best in class.</font>

36
00:02:32,354 --> 00:02:37,517
<font color="#000000">And the features are model-agnostic: adding them to
a plain GatedGCN cuts its ZINC error from 0.265 to 0.106,</font>

37
00:02:37,597 --> 00:02:42,760
<font color="#000000">all under a modest parameter budget.</font>

38
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:49,735
<font color="#000000">The lasting message: express a network's power as
which equivariant graph polynomials it can compute.</font>

39
00:02:49,815 --> 00:02:53,833
<font color="#000000">Each is a small multi-graph evaluated by a tensor
contraction,</font>

40
00:02:53,913 --> 00:02:58,921
<font color="#000000">a finer ruler than Weisfeiler-Lehman and a direct
list of missing operations.</font>

41
00:02:59,001 --> 00:03:00,243
<font color="#000000">Acting on that list,</font>

42
00:03:00,323 --> 00:03:06,257
<font color="#000000">precomputed polynomial features push ordinary graph
networks past three-WL to best-in-class</font>

43
00:03:06,337 --> 00:03:08,240
<font color="#000000">molecular property prediction.</font>
